{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md | head -180'","aggregated_output":"     1\t# Phase 2 plan: Axylo 1.0 in TypeScript\n     2\t\n     3\tRevision 3, 2026-11-18. Revised after three independent reviews ([ledger](../reviews/2026-11-18/ledger.md)) and the owner's decisions of the same day (scatter and a compact variant are in 1.0; the mark renderer is decided in M1).\n     4\t\n     5\tThis plan builds the product defined in [02-product-spec.md](02-product-spec.md). Criterion IDs (F1, L1, A2, DX4, P1 …) refer to that spec; finding IDs (R5, D2 …) refer to [01-landscape.md](01-landscape.md).\n     6\t\n     7\t**The release is done when every row of the acceptance matrix passes.** The matrix lives in the repository as data: one row per spec criterion and chart type, naming the test that covers it, with a result of pass, fail or not applicable (with the reason). The Phase 1 comparison is rerun with Axylo included and published, but it is comparison evidence, not the gate: several of its columns are descriptive, and it cannot run the performance budgets.\n     8\t\n     9\t## 1. Public API\n    10\t\n    11\tThe declarations are in [packages/axylo/src/public.ts](../packages/axylo/src/public.ts), and every example below is in [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts), type-checked against the package by `npm run typecheck`: the examples compile, and the deliberate mistakes fail to compile. (The first revision of this plan carried a separate declaration sketch, `docs/api/axylo.d.ts`; the package replaced it.)\n    12\t\n    13\t### 1.1 Shape\n    14\t\n    15\tOne function per chart type, each taking a target element and a plain options object, each returning the same small handle.\n    16\t\n    17\t```ts\n    18\timport { line } from 'axylo';\n    19\t\n    20\tconst chart = line('#sessions', {\n    21\t  title: 'Daily sessions by channel',\n    22\t  data: rows,              // [{ day: '2026-01-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 31713 }, …]\n    23\t  x: 'day',\n    24\t  y: 'sessions',\n    25\t  series: 'channel',\n    26\t});\n    27\t```\n    28\t\n    29\tThat is the whole first chart. Axes, legend, tooltip, keyboard access, description, data table, light and dark colors and resizing are on without further options.\n    30\t\n    31\t| Decision | Reason |\n    32\t|---|---|\n    33\t| Field names over a table | Finding D2: the ten libraries take five incompatible shapes, and the harness had to reshape its fixture a different way for each. Application data arrives as rows. |\n    34\t| One function per chart type (`line`, `area`, `bar`, `donut`, `scatter`) | Each has its own options type, so options that do not apply are type errors (DX3), and bundlers can drop unused chart types. Whether shared code actually tree-shakes is measured on real consumer builds in M7. |\n    35\t| Plain options object, not a builder or a JSX tree | The same object works in the React adapter. Recharts needed eight components for the trend task; the information content was four fields. |\n    36\t| No required configuration beyond data and fields | Findings R11, R15, D1: tooltips, legends, time adapters, margins, colors and accessibility were opt-in somewhere in every library. |\n    37\t\n    38\t### 1.2 Data: one logical table, three forms\n    39\t\n    40\t```ts\n    41\t// long rows: one row per (x, series)\n    42\tline(el, { data: rows, x: 'day', y: 'sessions', series: 'channel' });\n    43\t\n    44\t// wide rows: one column per series\n    45\tline(el, { data: wide, x: 'day', y: ['direct', 'organic', { field: 'referral', label: 'Referral' }] });\n    46\t\n    47\t// typed columns for large data; the row type is inferred from the columns\n    48\tline(el, { data: { columns: { t: new Float64Array(n), v: new Float32Array(n) } }, x: { field: 't', type: 'time' }, y: 'v' });\n    49\t```\n    50\t\n    51\tLong and wide are mutually exclusive in the types: `series` cannot be combined with an array of `y`.\n    52\t\n    53\tRules the three forms share:\n    54\t\n    55\t| Rule | Detail |\n    56\t|---|---|\n    57\t| Ownership | Row arrays are read once and never mutated. Typed columns are borrowed, not copied; the caller must not change them in place, and replaces them through `update`. |\n    58\t| Column lengths | All columns must have the same length; otherwise AX100. |\n    59\t| Missing values | Rows: `null`, or an absent key. Typed columns: `NaN` means missing and does not raise AX103. |\n    60\t| Order | Line, area and scatter are sorted by x internally (F11). `append` expects rows at or after the last x. |\n    61\t| Provenance | Every normalized sample keeps the index of the row it came from, so diagnostics, the table and events point at the caller's own data. |\n    62\t\n    63\t### 1.3 Fields\n    64\t\n    65\tA field is a key of the row, or an object when it needs more. The key is checked against the row type by role: a value axis accepts number fields; text fields are accepted there only with `parse: 'number'`.\n    66\t\n    67\t```ts\n    68\tline(el, {\n    69\t  data: samples,\n    70\t  x: { field: 'ts', type: 'time', timeZone: 'UTC' },\n    71\t  y: { field: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions', unit: 'sessions', tickFormat: { notation: 'compact' } },\n    72\t});\n    73\t\n    74\t// counts that a database driver returns as strings\n    75\tbar(el, { data: pgRows, x: 'day', y: { field: 'count', parse: 'number', missing: 'zero' } });\n    76\t```\n    77\t\n    78\t| Option | Meaning |\n    79\t|---|---|\n    80\t| `field` | Key of the row. |\n    81\t| `label`, `unit` | Axis title; name and unit in tooltip, table and announcements. |\n    82\t| `type` (x) | `'date'`, `'time'`, `'number'`, `'category'`. Inferred when omitted, except that a number is never inferred to be an instant (F4). |\n    83\t| `timeZone` (x) | For instants: `'local'` (default), `'UTC'`, or an IANA zone. |\n    84\t| `format`, `tickFormat` | Readouts and ticks are formatted separately (F6). |\n    85\t| `scale` (value) | `'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1). Declared, never inferred (F7). |\n    86\t| `missing` (value) | `'gap'` (default) or `'zero'` (F2). |\n    87\t| `parse` (value) | `'number'`, for a text field holding numbers (AX104, AX106). |\n    88\t| `domain` | Author bounds. Widened to zero on bar and area (F1). |\n    89\t\n    90\t### 1.4 Common options\n    91\t\n    92\tSummarized; the declarations are the reference.\n    93\t\n    94\t| Option | Purpose |\n    95\t|---|---|\n    96\t| `title`, `labelledBy`, `subtitle`, `description` | The chart's visible heading and accessible name (A1). `title` may carry a heading `level` and `visible: false`; `labelledBy` points at a heading the page already has. |\n    97\t| `state` | `'ready'`, `'loading'`, `{ error }`, `{ empty }` (L7). |\n    98\t| `legend`, `tooltip`, `table` | On by default; each can be turned off. |\n    99\t| `zoom`, `reference` | Cartesian charts only. |\n   100\t| `compact` | Line, area and bar: marks only, as a static image with a text alternative (spec 3.7). |\n   101\t| `colors` | A fixed color per series name (DX6). |\n   102\t| `messages`, `locale` | Built-in text and formatting locale (A10, F6). |\n   103\t| `strict`, `onDiagnostic` | Diagnostics delivery (section 3.5 of the spec). |\n   104\t\n   105\tThere is no `width`, `height`, `responsive`, `theme`, `palette` or `animation` option. Size comes from the container, colors from CSS, motion from the spec (I5). An unknown key raises AX150 with the closest known name.\n   106\t\n   107\t### 1.5 Handle and commit contract\n   108\t\n   109\t```ts\n   110\tchart.update({ title: 'Sessions' });                 // options\n   111\tchart.append([{ day: '2026-05-01', channel: 'Direct', sessions: 41210 }], { keep: 120 });   // rows\n   112\tchart.zoom(['2026-03-01', '2026-03-31']);            // view\n   113\tconst off = chart.on('hover', point => { … });\n   114\tchart.diagnostics;                                   // current, as soon as any call returns\n   115\tchart.destroy();\n   116\t```\n   117\t\n   118\tFinding D3: the ten libraries have seven update idioms. Axylo has three methods, one per kind of change, with one contract.\n   119\t\n   120\t| Rule | Detail |\n   121\t|---|---|\n   122\t| Order | Calls are applied in the order made. `update({ data })` followed by `append` appends to the new data. |\n   123\t| Two phases | Each call validates and normalizes synchronously: diagnostics are current when it returns, and a `strict` chart throws from that call. Drawing is deferred to the next animation frame, and calls made before that frame are drawn once. |\n   124\t| Atomic switch | Marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer move to a new data revision together. No frame mixes two revisions. |\n   125\t| Patches | A key present in an `update` replaces the old value whole; a key set to `undefined` returns to its default; absent keys stay. No deep merging. |\n   126\t| Rejected changes | A call that throws under `strict` leaves the chart on its last valid state. |\n   127\t| Streaming | `keep` is the number of distinct x values retained. Rows before the last x are accepted through the slower full update and raise AX151. While zoomed to the latest edge the view follows new data; zoomed elsewhere, it stays put. Paused charts buffer (I6). Keyboard focus stays on its datum until that datum leaves the window (A7). |\n   128\t| Teardown | `destroy()` cancels the pending frame, disconnects observers and listeners, and makes later calls no-ops. |\n   129\t\n   130\t### 1.6 Diagnostics\n   131\t\n   132\tThe registry, with each code's level and outcome, is in the spec (section 3.5). Example for the Phase 1 combined dirty fixture (`[10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70]`):\n   133\t\n   134\t```\n   135\t[axylo AX103] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" has 1 value that is not a finite number (NaN at row 3). Shown as a gap.\n   136\t  Check the calculation that produced it, or send null for \"no data\".\n   137\t[axylo AX106] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" mixes numbers with 1 string (\"40\" at row 4). The string is not plotted.\n   138\t  Convert it to a number where the data is produced.\n   139\t[axylo AX102] \"Dirty input\": y field \"value\" is absent in 1 row (row 5). Shown as a gap.\n   140\t  Send null if the value is known to be missing.\n   141\t```\n   142\t\n   143\tThe reader of that chart sees \"Values not shown: 3. See table.\" (F12).\n   144\t\n   145\t`validate(type, options)` runs the same normalization without a DOM and returns the diagnostics, so a server, a test or a coding agent can check a chart before rendering it.\n   146\t\n   147\tDevelopment and production flavours are selected through package `exports` conditions. The script-tag distribution ships `axylo.js` (development) and `axylo.min.js` (production). Errors and warnings print once per code per page in both; hints only in development.\n   148\t\n   149\t### 1.7 React adapter\n   150\t\n   151\t```tsx\n   152\timport { Line } from '@axylo/react';\n   153\t\n   154\t<Line data={rows} x=\"day\" y=\"sessions\" series=\"channel\" title=\"Daily sessions by channel\" />\n   155\t```\n   156\t\n   157\t| Behavior | Detail |\n   158\t|---|---|\n   159\t| Lifecycle | Create on mount, `destroy` on unmount; correct under StrictMode's setup, cleanup, setup. The file is marked `'use client'`. |\n   160\t| Props to options | Field specs are compared structurally, so an inline `x={{ field: 'ts' }}` does not renormalize on every render. `data` is compared by identity; in development, the same content arriving three times with a new identity raises a hint to memoize it. |\n   161\t| Removed props | A prop that disappears is sent as `undefined`, which restores the default. |\n   162\t| Handlers | Replaced without recreating the chart. |\n   163\t| Streaming | A ref exposes the handle, so `append`, `zoom` and `diagnostics` are reachable. When `data` is controlled, a new `data` prop replaces whatever was appended. |\n   164\t\n   165\tThe adapter is prototyped in M3, before the API freezes, because it is the cheapest way to find out whether the core contract survives a second consumer. It ships in 1.0 because finding 1.1 of the landscape shows where everyday charts are written; that is evidence of where charts are written, not of demand for Axylo.\n   166\t\n   167\t## 2. Architecture\n   168\t\n   169\t### 2.1 Pipeline\n   170\t\n   171\t```\n   172\toptions ─► normalize ─► frame ──┬─► view model ─► layout ─► scene ─► mark renderer\n   173\t            │ validate   (authoritative) │   scales, visible range         text renderer (DOM)\n   174\t            ▼ diagnostics                ├─► lookup: exact values by x, by point, by ordinal\n   175\t                                         ├─► summary and table\n   176\t                                         └─► focus model\n   177\t```\n   178\t\n   179\tThe **frame** is the normalized, lossless data: typed columns, a validity bitmap, dictionary-encoded categories, source-row indexes and a revision number. It is the only source for exact values. The table, the summary, keyboard navigation, tooltips and events read the frame. The **scene** is geometry for drawing and may be simplified (F5); nothing reads values back from it. The first draft derived accessibility from the scene, and two reviewers showed that a simplified drawing cannot return the samples it dropped.\n   180\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
