{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_40","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"head -35 packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/EVIDENCE.json && cat tests/dist/tsconfig.json && rg -n 'heading-order|scrollable-region-focusable' packages/axylo/test/browser/a11y/axe.browser.test.ts && cat docs/decisions/0004* && head -140 packages/axylo/src/theme/styles.ts\"","aggregated_output":"[\n  {\n    \"criterion\": \"A1\",\n    \"type\": \"line\",\n    \"file\": \"packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts\",\n    \"title\": \"A1 line: the title is the accessible name and the description is the generated summary: type, series, x and y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"criterion\": \"A1\",\n    \"type\": \"line\",\n    \"file\": \"packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts\",\n    \"title\": \"A1 line: with no title and no labelledBy the name is generated from type and measure (\\\"Line chart of Energy…\\\") and AX140 is raised (development build)\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"criterion\": \"A1\",\n    \"type\": \"line\",\n    \"file\": \"packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts\",\n    \"title\": \"A1 line: with no title and no labelledBy the name is generated from type and measure (\\\"Line chart of Energy…\\\") and AX140 is raised (production build)\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"criterion\": \"A1\",\n    \"type\": \"line\",\n    \"file\": \"packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts\",\n    \"title\": \"A1 line: labelledBy names the chart from the page's own heading, without a heading of its own and without AX140\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"criterion\": \"A1\",\n    \"type\": \"line\",\n    \"file\": \"packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts\",\n    \"title\": \"A1 line: the title's heading level is the author's, and a visually hidden title still names the chart\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"criterion\": \"A1\",\n    \"type\": \"line\",\n    \"file\": \"packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a1-names.browser.test.ts\",\n{\n  \"extends\": \"../../tsconfig.base.json\",\n  \"compilerOptions\": { \"noEmit\": true, \"types\": [\"node\"] },\n  \"include\": [\"*.ts\", \"pages/*.ts\"]\n}\n67:        expect(ran).toContain('heading-order');\n69:        if (state === 'table open' && fixture === 'trend') expect(ran).toContain('scrollable-region-focusable');\n90:      expect(ran, state).toContain('heading-order');\n# Decision 0004: where the tooltip goes, and when the focused point's text changes\n\nStatus: decided by the coordinator on 2026-11-18 after the second review of the interaction layer (`reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/core3.interaction.review.md`, new findings 3 and 4). Not rehearsed with a screen reader or a screen magnifier; both parts need that before they are called verified.\n\n## 1. The tooltip is offset across the direction of reading, not along it\n\n**Problem.** WCAG 1.4.13 requires content shown on hover to be hoverable: the pointer must be able to move onto it without it disappearing. Spec I1 requires the readout to name the datum nearest the pointer on every move. On a line chart the whole plot is the picking surface, so a tooltip placed beside the pointer is entered by ordinary left-to-right reading: the plot stops receiving moves and the readout stalls on the old datum. The first fix (12 px, then 22 px of clearance) made the outcome depend on pointer speed: a single move longer than the clearance still lands on the tooltip.\n\n**Rule.**\n\n1. A chart is read along its position axis (x for line, area, scatter and vertical bars; y for horizontal bars). The tooltip is placed on the other side of the pointer **across** that axis: above the pointer for a horizontal position axis (below when there is no room above), to the right for a vertical one (left when there is no room). Along the position axis it is centred on the active mark and shifted only as far as needed to stay inside the plot and every clipping ancestor.\n2. It keeps a clearance from the pointer and from the active mark, so moving along the position axis never enters it, at any speed. Moving straight toward it does not change the nearest datum (the position along the axis is unchanged), so the pointer reaches it with the same content showing: that is what makes it hoverable.\n3. While the pointer is on the tooltip, the datum is held. Leaving it back into the plot resumes picking; leaving it elsewhere closes it after the usual delay. Escape dismisses it without moving the pointer.\n4. When neither side across the axis has room (short tiles, compact layouts), the fixed readout under the plot is used instead. The readout has reserved space: it never covers the controls and is never pointer-transparent over them.\n5. Donut and other two-dimensional picks have no reading axis: the tooltip goes outside the ring toward the nearer edge of the plot, or to the fixed readout when it does not fit.\n6. A pinned tooltip (click, tap or keyboard) follows the same placement and stays until dismissed or until its datum leaves the displayed frame.\n\nRejected: making the tooltip transparent to the pointer (fails \"hoverable\"); a larger clearance alone (still speed-dependent); always using the fixed readout (loses the association between the readout and the mark on large plots, which is the main use of a tooltip).\n\n## 2. The focused point's text changes only for a reason the reader would name\n\n**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n\n**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n\n1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n4. the plot regains focus.\n\nAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n\nTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n/**\n * Applies the default stylesheet (plan 2.4) to the root a chart lives in: its document, or the shadow root\n * that contains it (a document's sheets do not reach into a shadow tree).\n *\n * - Normally a constructable stylesheet is adopted (`adoptedStyleSheets`); no `<style>` element is created.\n * - A root that already has the standalone dist/axylo.css active gets nothing. \"Active\" is checked by its\n *   effect (a probe element picks up the sheet's own rules), not by trusting a sentinel property, and is\n *   checked again on later calls, so a sheet that was removed or disabled is replaced.\n * - Only where constructable or adopted stylesheets are missing is one `<style>` element inserted instead.\n *   This is the only case in which the library creates a style element.\n */\nimport { AXYLO_CSS } from './css';\n\ntype StyleRoot = Document | ShadowRoot;\n\ntype Installed =\n  | { readonly kind: 'adopted'; readonly sheet: CSSStyleSheet }\n  | { readonly kind: 'element'; readonly element: HTMLStyleElement }\n  | { readonly kind: 'standalone'; readonly sheet: CSSStyleSheet | null };\n\nconst installed = new WeakMap<StyleRoot, Installed>();\n/** One constructed sheet per document, shared by its document and shadow roots. */\nconst constructed = new WeakMap<Document, CSSStyleSheet>();\n\n/**\n * Makes the default styles apply to `root` (a Document or a ShadowRoot). Safe to call any number of times;\n * after the first call it costs a few property reads unless the sheet it relied on has gone away.\n */\nexport function ensureStyles(root: StyleRoot): void {\n  const state = installed.get(root);\n  if (state !== undefined && stillActive(root, state)) return;\n  const doc = 'ownerDocument' in root && root.ownerDocument !== null ? root.ownerDocument : (root as Document);\n  const win = doc.defaultView;\n  if (win === null) return;\n\n  const standalone = findStandalone(root, doc, win);\n  if (standalone !== undefined) { installed.set(root, { kind: 'standalone', sheet: standalone }); return; }\n\n  if (canAdopt(root, win)) {\n    let sheet = constructed.get(doc);\n    if (sheet === undefined) {\n      // The constructor must come from the document's own window, or adopting the sheet throws.\n      sheet = new win.CSSStyleSheet();\n      sheet.replaceSync(AXYLO_CSS);\n      constructed.set(doc, sheet);\n    }\n    const list = (root as { adoptedStyleSheets: CSSStyleSheet[] }).adoptedStyleSheets;\n    if (!list.includes(sheet)) (root as { adoptedStyleSheets: CSSStyleSheet[] }).adoptedStyleSheets = [...list, sheet];\n    installed.set(root, { kind: 'adopted', sheet });\n    return;\n  }\n\n  const element = doc.createElement('style');\n  element.setAttribute('data-axylo-styles', '');\n  element.textContent = AXYLO_CSS;\n  if (root === doc) (doc.head ?? doc.documentElement).appendChild(element);\n  else root.insertBefore(element, root.firstChild);\n  installed.set(root, { kind: 'element', element });\n}\n\nfunction stillActive(root: StyleRoot, state: Installed): boolean {\n  switch (state.kind) {\n    case 'adopted': return (root as { adoptedStyleSheets?: CSSStyleSheet[] }).adoptedStyleSheets?.includes(state.sheet) ?? false;\n    case 'element': return state.element.isConnected && state.element.sheet !== null && !state.element.sheet.disabled;\n    case 'standalone': {\n      if (state.sheet === null) return false; // not identifiable: verify by effect again\n      const owner = state.sheet.ownerNode;\n      return !state.sheet.disabled && owner !== null && owner.isConnected && Array.prototype.includes.call(root.styleSheets, state.sheet);\n    }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction canAdopt(root: StyleRoot, win: Window): boolean {\n  if (!('adoptedStyleSheets' in root)) return false;\n  const Sheet = (win as unknown as { CSSStyleSheet?: { new(): CSSStyleSheet; prototype: Partial<CSSStyleSheet> } }).CSSStyleSheet;\n  if (typeof Sheet !== 'function' || typeof Sheet.prototype.replaceSync !== 'function') return false;\n  try { new Sheet(); } catch { return false; }\n  return true;\n}\n\n/**\n * The standalone sheet if it is in effect in `root`: undefined if not, null if in effect but not\n * identifiable (a cross-origin sheet whose rules cannot be read). In effect means a probe chart root picks\n * up a private token and a mark rule that only this stylesheet defines; custom properties alone could be\n * inherited from outside a shadow root, so a non-inherited property is checked as well.\n */\nfunction findStandalone(root: StyleRoot, doc: Document, win: Window): CSSStyleSheet | null | undefined {\n  const probe = doc.createElement('div');\n  probe.className = 'axylo';\n  probe.style.setProperty('display', 'none');\n  const mark = doc.createElement('div');\n  mark.className = 'axylo-svg';\n  probe.appendChild(mark);\n  (root === doc ? doc.documentElement : root).appendChild(probe);\n  const token = win.getComputedStyle(probe).getPropertyValue('--axylo-default-background').trim();\n  const rule = win.getComputedStyle(mark).position;\n  probe.remove();\n  if (token === '' || rule !== 'absolute') return undefined;\n  for (const sheet of Array.from(root.styleSheets)) {\n    try {\n      for (const r of Array.from(sheet.cssRules)) if (r.cssText.includes('--axylo-stylesheet')) return sheet;\n    } catch {\n      // Cross-origin: its rules cannot be read.\n    }\n  }\n  return null;\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
