ion is a refit with byte-level checks and corrected text. The hard constraints are measured and tested. Whether the result is good to look at is the owner's call (see \"For the owner\"). The refit itself has not been reviewed.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:34:| Byte-rounded simulation, worst pair | 0.098 (light protan 6–8) | 0.102 |\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:51:The same charts are repeated under the three simulations (the Machado matrices as SVG color matrices in linear RGB) and in grayscale. Source: `research/palette/preview.html`.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:57:- **Analytic.** The 8-bit hex is decoded, then simulated or composited in floating point, then converted to OKLab.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:58:- **Bytes.** Values are rounded where an 8-bit image would round them. A simulated color is encoded and rounded to the nearest byte before the OKLab conversion; that is what the simulated rows of the preview PNGs hold. A translucent fill is quantised as a raster would quantise it: alpha at round(α×255), each channel rounded. That reproduces Chrome 157's canvas and SVG fills on white exactly and is within one byte on #16181d. The second variant, alpha at floor(α×255), is what the review's CPU Skia raster did.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:71:| 2, bytes | the same after the simulation is rounded to bytes | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 |\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:145:The first version said that each simulation \"removes one chromatic axis\", and that three deep colors were therefore forced. Both statements were wrong.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:147:The Machado matrices act in linear RGB, not in OKLab. In OKLab they mostly compress the red–green direction (protanopia, deuteranopia) or the blue–yellow direction (tritanopia), but they also move lightness. For example, the first version's light blue has L 0.659 unsimulated and between 0.643 and 0.691 across the simulations. The compression is a design heuristic, not a proof.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:181:- **Byte-level checks** (tested): byte-rounded simulation; fills on both 8-bit raster models; Chrome's drawn pixels within one byte of the model and 0.06 apart.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:182:- **Ring closure and deficiency-simulated fills** (recorded only). At 25% the donut's closing pairs go down to 0.032 (light 8–1) and 0.037 (dark 6–1). Neighbours under simulation go down to 0.024 (light, deuteranopia). Slices are opaque in Axylo, so constraint 2 covers them.\ndocs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md:213:- The simulation is Machado at severity 1.0 only.\nrg: node_modules/playwright-core/lib/server/screenshotter.js: No such file or directory (os error 2)\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:13651:     * - finite animations are fast-forwarded to completion, so they'll fire `transitionend` event.\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:13656:    animations?: \"disabled\"|\"allow\";\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:16717:   * await page.getByRole('link').screenshot({ animations: 'disabled', path: 'link.png' });\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:26132:   * - finite animations are fast-forwarded to completion, so they'll fire `transitionend` event.\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:26137:  animations?: \"disabled\"|\"allow\";\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:26855:   * - finite animations are fast-forwarded to completion, so they'll fire `transitionend` event.\nnode_modules/playwright-core/types/types.d.ts:26860:  animations?: \"disabled\"|\"allow\";\nls: cannot access '/usr/share/nodejs': No such file or directory\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
