{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_55","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'nl -ba packages/axylo/test/browser/chart/util.ts | head -86 && git show 13b2f63:packages/axylo/test/browser/chart/util.ts | nl -ba | head -80 && nl -ba docs/decisions/0001* | head -57 && nl -ba docs/decisions/0004* | tail -13'","aggregated_output":"     1\t/**\n     2\t * Shared setup for the chart tests: the test page with the built script-tag bundle (`window.Axylo`, the\n     3\t * development flavour), and the Phase 1 fixtures (research/harness/src/data.js) installed as `window.fx`.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport type { BrowserContextOptions, Page } from 'playwright-core';\n     6\timport { expect } from 'vitest';\n     7\timport { openPage } from '../harness';\n     8\timport { decodePng, type Image } from '../render/png';\n     9\t\n    10\texport type { Image };\n    11\t\n    12\tdeclare global {\n    13\t  interface Window {\n    14\t    // The bundle's global and the fixtures, typed loosely: the tests drive the public API as a page would.\n    15\t    Axylo: Record<string, (...args: unknown[]) => ChartLike>;\n    16\t    fx: Fixtures;\n    17\t    charts: ChartLike[];\n    18\t    nextFrames(n?: number): Promise<void>;\n    19\t  }\n    20\t}\n    21\t\n    22\texport interface ChartLike {\n    23\t  update(patch: Record<string, unknown>): void;\n    24\t  append(rows: readonly unknown[], options?: { keep?: number }): void;\n    25\t  zoom(domain: readonly unknown[] | null): void;\n    26\t  on(event: string, listener: (payload: unknown) => void): () => void;\n    27\t  readonly diagnostics: readonly { code: string; level: string; message: string; field?: string; chart: string }[];\n    28\t  destroy(): void;\n    29\t  __bench: { revision: number; ready(): Promise<void>; describe(): { xDomain: number[] | null; yDomain: number[] | null; series: { key: string; points: number; oldestX: number; newestX: number; missing: number }[] } };\n    30\t  __dev: { stages(): Stages; fail(stage: string | null): void; renderer: string };\n    31\t}\n    32\t\n    33\t/** Counters per pipeline stage (src/core/chart.ts, STAGES). */\n    34\texport type Stages = Record<'options' | 'normalize' | 'append' | 'view' | 'frame' | 'theme' | 'formatters' | 'layout' | 'scene' | 'lookup' | 'reader' | 'draw' | 'text' | 'figure', number>;\n    35\t\n    36\texport interface Fixtures {\n    37\t  trend: { title: string; yTitle: string; t: number[]; series: { name: string; values: (number | null)[] }[] };\n    38\t  trendRows(): Record<string, unknown>[];\n    39\t  datesRows(): Record<string, unknown>[];\n    40\t  bars: { title: string; yTitle: string; name: string; categories: string[]; values: number[] };\n    41\t  parts: { title: string; name: string; labels: string[]; values: number[] };\n    42\t  dirtyRows(): Record<string, unknown>[];\n    43\t  scale(n: number): { title: string; t: number[]; y: number[] };\n    44\t  rng(seed: number): () => number;\n    45\t}\n    46\t\n    47\t/** Runs in the page: the Phase 1 fixtures, reproduced from research/harness/src/data.js. */\n    48\tfunction installFixtures(): void {\n    49\t  function rng(seed: number) {\n    50\t    let s = seed >>> 0;\n    51\t    return () => { s = (s + 0x6D2B79F5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(s ^ (s >>> 15), 1 | s); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };\n    52\t  }\n    53\t  const DAY = 864e5;\n    54\t  const START = Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1);\n    55\t  const trend = (() => {\n    56\t    const r = rng(7), n = 120;\n    57\t    const t = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => START + i * DAY);\n    58\t    const mk = (base: number, growth: number, noise: number, season: number) => t.map((_, i) => Math.round(base + growth * i + season * Math.sin((i / 7) * 2 * Math.PI) + (r() - 0.5) * noise));\n    59\t    const series: { name: string; values: (number | null)[] }[] = [\n    60\t      { name: 'Direct', values: mk(31000, 120, 4000, 3000) },\n    61\t      { name: 'Organic search', values: mk(18000, 60, 3000, 2500) },\n    62\t      { name: 'Referral', values: mk(4200, -8, 1200, 400) },\n    63\t    ];\n    64\t    for (let i = 60; i < 67; i++) series[2]!.values[i] = null;\n    65\t    return { title: 'Daily sessions by channel', yTitle: 'Sessions', t, series };\n    66\t  })();\n    67\t  const days = trend.t.map(t => new Date(t).toISOString().slice(0, 10));\n    68\t  const dirty = { categories: ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun'], values: [10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70] };\n    69\t  const fx = {\n    70\t    rng,\n    71\t    trend,\n    72\t    trendRows: () => trend.series.flatMap(s => s.values.map((v, i) => ({ day: trend.t[i], channel: s.name, sessions: v }))),\n    73\t    datesRows: () => trend.series.flatMap(s => s.values.map((v, i) => ({ day: days[i], channel: s.name, sessions: v }))),\n    74\t    bars: {\n    75\t      title: 'Customer satisfaction by team', yTitle: 'Satisfied (%)', name: 'Satisfied (%)',\n    76\t      categories: ['Billing', 'Customer Success & Onboarding', 'Platform', 'Mobile', 'Data', 'Security', 'Sales Eng.'],\n    77\t      values: [94, 88, 97, 82, 91, 85, 90],\n    78\t    },\n    79\t    parts: {\n    80\t      title: 'Sessions by browser', name: 'Share (%)',\n    81\t      labels: ['Chrome', 'Safari', 'Edge', 'Firefox', 'Samsung Internet', 'Other'],\n    82\t      values: [46.2, 27.5, 14.1, 8.9, 2.1, 1.2],\n    83\t    },\n    84\t    dirtyRows: () => dirty.categories.map((day, i) => (dirty.values[i] === undefined ? { day } : { day, value: dirty.values[i] })),\n    85\t    scale(n: number) {\n    86\t      const r = rng(1), t = new Array<number>(n), y = new Array<number>(n);\n     1\t/**\n     2\t * Shared setup for the chart tests: the test page with the built script-tag bundle (`window.Axylo`, the\n     3\t * development flavour), and the Phase 1 fixtures (research/harness/src/data.js) installed as `window.fx`.\n     4\t */\n     5\timport type { BrowserContextOptions, Page } from 'playwright-core';\n     6\timport { openPage } from '../harness';\n     7\timport { decodePng, type Image } from '../render/png';\n     8\t\n     9\texport type { Image };\n    10\t\n    11\tdeclare global {\n    12\t  interface Window {\n    13\t    // The bundle's global and the fixtures, typed loosely: the tests drive the public API as a page would.\n    14\t    Axylo: Record<string, (...args: unknown[]) => ChartLike>;\n    15\t    fx: Fixtures;\n    16\t    charts: ChartLike[];\n    17\t    nextFrames(n?: number): Promise<void>;\n    18\t  }\n    19\t}\n    20\t\n    21\texport interface ChartLike {\n    22\t  update(patch: Record<string, unknown>): void;\n    23\t  append(rows: readonly unknown[], options?: { keep?: number }): void;\n    24\t  zoom(domain: readonly unknown[] | null): void;\n    25\t  on(event: string, listener: (payload: unknown) => void): () => void;\n    26\t  readonly diagnostics: readonly { code: string; level: string; message: string; field?: string; chart: string }[];\n    27\t  destroy(): void;\n    28\t  __bench: { revision: number; ready(): Promise<void>; describe(): { xDomain: number[] | null; yDomain: number[] | null; series: { key: string; points: number; oldestX: number; newestX: number; missing: number }[] } };\n    29\t  __dev: { stages(): Stages; fail(stage: string | null): void; renderer: string };\n    30\t}\n    31\t\n    32\t/** Counters per pipeline stage (src/core/chart.ts, STAGES). */\n    33\texport type Stages = Record<'options' | 'normalize' | 'append' | 'view' | 'frame' | 'theme' | 'formatters' | 'layout' | 'scene' | 'lookup' | 'reader' | 'draw' | 'text' | 'figure', number>;\n    34\t\n    35\texport interface Fixtures {\n    36\t  trend: { title: string; yTitle: string; t: number[]; series: { name: string; values: (number | null)[] }[] };\n    37\t  trendRows(): Record<string, unknown>[];\n    38\t  datesRows(): Record<string, unknown>[];\n    39\t  bars: { title: string; yTitle: string; name: string; categories: string[]; values: number[] };\n    40\t  parts: { title: string; name: string; labels: string[]; values: number[] };\n    41\t  dirtyRows(): Record<string, unknown>[];\n    42\t  scale(n: number): { title: string; t: number[]; y: number[] };\n    43\t  rng(seed: number): () => number;\n    44\t}\n    45\t\n    46\t/** Runs in the page: the Phase 1 fixtures, reproduced from research/harness/src/data.js. */\n    47\tfunction installFixtures(): void {\n    48\t  function rng(seed: number) {\n    49\t    let s = seed >>> 0;\n    50\t    return () => { s = (s + 0x6D2B79F5) | 0; let t = Math.imul(s ^ (s >>> 15), 1 | s); t = (t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), 61 | t)) ^ t; return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };\n    51\t  }\n    52\t  const DAY = 864e5;\n    53\t  const START = Date.UTC(2026, 0, 1);\n    54\t  const trend = (() => {\n    55\t    const r = rng(7), n = 120;\n    56\t    const t = Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => START + i * DAY);\n    57\t    const mk = (base: number, growth: number, noise: number, season: number) => t.map((_, i) => Math.round(base + growth * i + season * Math.sin((i / 7) * 2 * Math.PI) + (r() - 0.5) * noise));\n    58\t    const series: { name: string; values: (number | null)[] }[] = [\n    59\t      { name: 'Direct', values: mk(31000, 120, 4000, 3000) },\n    60\t      { name: 'Organic search', values: mk(18000, 60, 3000, 2500) },\n    61\t      { name: 'Referral', values: mk(4200, -8, 1200, 400) },\n    62\t    ];\n    63\t    for (let i = 60; i < 67; i++) series[2]!.values[i] = null;\n    64\t    return { title: 'Daily sessions by channel', yTitle: 'Sessions', t, series };\n    65\t  })();\n    66\t  const days = trend.t.map(t => new Date(t).toISOString().slice(0, 10));\n    67\t  const dirty = { categories: ['Mon', 'Tue', 'Wed', 'Thu', 'Fri', 'Sat', 'Sun'], values: [10, 20, null, NaN, '40', undefined, 70] };\n    68\t  const fx = {\n    69\t    rng,\n    70\t    trend,\n    71\t    trendRows: () => trend.series.flatMap(s => s.values.map((v, i) => ({ day: trend.t[i], channel: s.name, sessions: v }))),\n    72\t    datesRows: () => trend.series.flatMap(s => s.values.map((v, i) => ({ day: days[i], channel: s.name, sessions: v }))),\n    73\t    bars: {\n    74\t      title: 'Customer satisfaction by team', yTitle: 'Satisfied (%)', name: 'Satisfied (%)',\n    75\t      categories: ['Billing', 'Customer Success & Onboarding', 'Platform', 'Mobile', 'Data', 'Security', 'Sales Eng.'],\n    76\t      values: [94, 88, 97, 82, 91, 85, 90],\n    77\t    },\n    78\t    parts: {\n    79\t      title: 'Sessions by browser', name: 'Share (%)',\n    80\t      labels: ['Chrome', 'Safari', 'Edge', 'Firefox', 'Samsung Internet', 'Other'],\n     1\t# Decision 0001: how the plot is exposed to keyboards and screen readers\n     2\t\n     3\tStatus: working choice, **not rehearsed with any screen reader**. The plan (M1) called for three candidate patterns to be rehearsed on five screen-reader and browser pairs before choosing. No screen reader can be run in the environment this was built in, so the choice below was made by reasoning and by inspecting Chrome's accessibility tree, and it must be rehearsed by the owner before it is relied on. If rehearsal contradicts it, the plot's roles are internal and can change without touching the public API.\n     4\t\n     5\t## Candidates\n     6\t\n     7\t| Pattern | For | Against |\n     8\t|---|---|---|\n     9\t| A. `role=\"application\"` on the plot, arrow keys handled in script, point text sent to a live region | Used by Recharts and ApexCharts. Full control of keys. | Screen-reader authors discourage `application`; a live region is a second, slower channel; touch screen readers have no arrow keys, so points cannot be reached by swiping. |\n    10\t| B. A roving focusable proxy element per point (or one moved around), each with its own label | Each point is a real element with a name. | One element per point does not scale to 100,000 points; a single moved proxy re-announces its role on every move in some readers (from memory, not verified). |\n    11\t| C. One native `<input type=\"range\">` laid over the plot, its value the position along x, its `aria-valuetext` the point's text | A native control: desktop readers enter focus mode on it by themselves, arrow keys, Home, End and Page keys work without script, and a change of value text is read without a live region. Touch screen readers expose a native range as an adjustable element, so swiping up and down moves through points (from memory for VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack; not verified here). One element whatever the number of points. | It is announced as a slider, which is not what a chart is; mitigated by `aria-roledescription` and a label that says what moving it does. Up and Down natively do the same as Right and Left. |\n    12\t\n    13\t## Choice: C\n    14\t\n    15\tOne visually transparent native range input covers the plot. It is the plot's single tab stop.\n    16\t\n    17\t- **Value**: the ordinal of the current x among the visible x positions (for scatter: among visible points in x then y order; for donut: slice order), from `Lookup.ordinal` and `Lookup.count`.\n    18\t- **Value text**: by default everything at that x, like the pointer tooltip: the x, then each visible series with its value, then \"point 4 of 120\". After the reader narrows to one series (Up or Down on a keyboard), only that series is read. Escape returns to all series.\n    19\t- **Keys**: Left and Right move along x (native). Home, End, Page Up and Page Down native. Up and Down are intercepted to change the series in focus. `+`, `-`, `0` and Shift+arrows zoom and pan. Enter or Space selects (fires `select`). Escape: closes the tooltip first, then leaves single-series mode; otherwise it is not consumed, so a chart inside a dialog does not swallow it.\n    20\t- **Touch screen readers**: swiping up or down on the adjustable element moves along x and reads every series at that x; the table is one control away in the toolbar.\n    21\t- **Everything else** (legend toggles, zoom buttons, pause, table) is ordinary buttons in one toolbar with roving focus: the chart's second and last tab stop while the table is closed.\n    22\t\n    23\t## What has to be checked by rehearsal\n    24\t\n    25\t1. NVDA with Chrome and with Firefox: focus mode is entered on the range without a keystroke; each arrow press reads the new value text once; Up and Down are passed through and the series change is read.\n    26\t2. VoiceOver with Safari on macOS: the same, and the role description is read sensibly.\n    27\t3. VoiceOver on iOS and TalkBack with Chrome: the element is reachable by touch exploration at the plot's position; swipe up and down move by one point and read it; the toolbar and table are reachable by swiping right.\n    28\t4. That a long value text (eight series) is not cut off, and that streaming data does not make the reader speak unless the reader moves.\n    21\t\n    22\t**Problem.** The plot's keyboard proxy is a native range whose value text describes the focused point, ending with its position (\"Point 4 of 120\"). While data streams in, the count changes on every append, so the text of a focused control changes without the reader acting. Spec A7 forbids unrequested announcements; whether a given screen reader speaks such a change cannot be tested here.\n    23\t\n    24\t**Rule.** The range's bounds and numeric value are always kept true to the displayed frame, silently. Its value text is rewritten only when:\n    25\t\n    26\t1. the reader moves (a key, an assistive adjustment, a pointer or touch selection that takes focus),\n    27\t2. the focused datum's own values change (the same x and series now read differently),\n    28\t3. the focused datum leaves the frame (the text must then say where focus went), or\n    29\t4. the plot regains focus.\n    30\t\n    31\tAn append that only changes the count or the ordinal of an unchanged datum does not rewrite the text. The position in the text can therefore be out of date until the next of the four events; it is recomputed then.\n    32\t\n    33\tTo check with a screen reader when one is available (added to decision 0001's list): idle appends with the plot focused are silent; an eviction of the focused point is spoken once; an increment immediately after an append lands on the expected neighbour and reads the current count.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
