{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_63","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"git show 13b2f63:packages/axylo/src/public.ts | rg -n 'seeTable|jumpNotFound|plotInstructions' && nl -ba docs/decisions/0003-default-palette.md | sed -n '55,72p' && nl -ba packages/axylo/test/browser/a11y/util.ts | tail -72 | head -20\"","aggregated_output":"203:  panLeft: string; panRight: string; jumpNotFound: string; plotInstructions: string;\n204:  /** Fills `{seeTable}` in `valuesNotShown`, `valuesChanged`, `labelsHidden` and `seriesNotDrawn` when the chart has a table; without one the placeholder is removed. An override without the placeholder is used as written. */\n205:  seeTable: string;\n    55\tThere are three forms of measurement:\n    56\t\n    57\t- **Analytic.** The 8-bit hex is decoded, then simulated or composited in floating point, then converted to OKLab.\n    58\t- **Bytes.** Values are rounded where an 8-bit image would round them. A simulated color is encoded and rounded to the nearest byte before the OKLab conversion; that is what the simulated rows of the preview PNGs hold. A translucent fill is quantised as a raster would quantise it: alpha at round(α×255), each channel rounded. That reproduces Chrome 157's canvas and SVG fills on white exactly and is within one byte on #16181d. The second variant, alpha at floor(α×255), is what the review's CPU Skia raster did.\n    59\t- **Chrome.** The fill pixels Chrome actually drew, canvas and SVG (`research/palette/raster.mjs`).\n    60\t\n    61\tThe unit test requires the analytic and the byte forms. It checks the recorded Chrome pixels against the 0.06 limit, and checks that each recorded pixel is within one byte of the model. A stricter margin is the worst case over every byte color within one byte per channel of both fills. It is recorded but not required, because I found no palette that meets it together with the rest (0.054–0.056 is the best I saw; see below).\n    62\t\n    63\t## Constraints, measured\n    64\t\n    65\tThese come from `research/palette/palette.json`, which `score.mjs` writes. The unit test recomputes each value with its own code and compares it at the written precision, including which pair is closest.\n    66\t\n    67\t| Constraint | Check | Limit | Light | Dark |\n    68\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n    69\t| 1 | lowest contrast on background | ≥ 3 | 3.03 (slate) | 3.26 (purple) |\n    70\t| 2 | min pairwise ΔE, all 8, normal / protan / deutan / tritan | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.104 / 0.103 / 0.102 |\n    71\t| 2, bytes | the same after the simulation is rounded to bytes | ≥ 0.10 | 0.124 / 0.103 / 0.103 / 0.102 | 0.116 / 0.103 / 0.102 / 0.102 |\n    72\t| 3 | first 4, worst vision (analytic; bytes) | ≥ 0.14 | 0.156 protan; 0.155 | 0.158 tritan; 0.159 |\n   114\t\n   115\texport interface AxeViolation { id: string; impact: string | null; nodes: string[]; help: string }\n   116\t\n   117\t/** axe-core over `selector` (every rule axe enables by default, best practices such as heading-order included). */\n   118\texport async function axe(page: Page, selector = 'body'): Promise<{ violations: AxeViolation[]; ran: string[]; incomplete: string[] }> {\n   119\t  if (!(await page.evaluate(() => 'axe' in window))) await page.addScriptTag({ content: axeSource });\n   120\t  return page.evaluate(async selector => {\n   121\t    type Rule = { id: string; impact: string | null; help: string; nodes: { target: string[] }[] };\n   122\t    const run = (window as unknown as { axe: { run(context: unknown, options: unknown): Promise<{ violations: Rule[]; passes: Rule[]; incomplete: Rule[] }> } }).axe;\n   123\t    const result = await run.run(selector, { resultTypes: ['violations', 'passes', 'incomplete'] });\n   124\t    return {\n   125\t      violations: result.violations.map(v => ({ id: v.id, impact: v.impact, help: v.help, nodes: v.nodes.map(n => n.target.join(' ')) })),\n   126\t      // A rule axe could not decide is not a r